const workedSolutions = [
  "Use a 12 V extra-low-voltage source, set the output to 0 V before connection, set a 100 mA current limit, verify lead polarity with the output disabled, inspect for shorts, then raise voltage while watching current. Stop on current limit, heating, odor, or an unexpected rail. Record the discharged-state resistance only after confirming capacitors are safe.",
  "4.7 kΩ = 4.7×10³ Ω = 4700 Ω; 220 nF = 220×10⁻⁹ F = 2.20×10⁻⁷ F; 3.3 mA = 3.3×10⁻³ A. A 4.7 kΩ ±5% resistor spans 4465–4935 Ω before meter uncertainty. If the meter adds ±(0.8% of reading + 2 digits), calculate that separately and report the combined bounded interval rather than extra decimal places.",
  "The resistor must drop 9−2 = 7 V at 10 mA, so R = 7/0.010 = 700 Ω. The next conservative E12 value is 750 Ω, giving about 9.33 mA. Resistor dissipation is I²R ≈ 0.065 W, so a 0.25 W part has comfortable margin. Recalculate using LED forward-voltage limits, not only 2.0 V typical.",
  "Temperature rise is 1.2 W × 45 °C/W = 54 °C. At 30 °C ambient the estimated junction temperature is 84 °C. This is acceptable only if it remains below the recommended operating limit with interface and airflow uncertainty; absolute maximum junction temperature is not a design target.",
  "Measure the 3.3 V rail with a DMM in DC-voltage mode connected in parallel. Measure 20 mA by opening the branch and inserting the meter in series on an appropriate fused current range. Use a compensated ×10 probe and stable edge trigger for 100 kHz PWM. Use a logic analyzer with a shared low-voltage reference for I²C, then verify voltage levels with the oscilloscope.",
  "A 30 kΩ/10 kΩ divider gives 3.00 V unloaded from 12 V. A 100 kΩ load makes the lower leg 10 kΩ || 100 kΩ = 9.09 kΩ, so output becomes 12×9.09/(30+9.09) = 2.79 V. This 7% loading error shows why divider current, load impedance, power, and tolerance must be evaluated together.",
  "Choose ground, assign each branch current direction, and write one KCL equation per nonreference node. A negative solved current means the real direction is opposite the assumed arrow, not that conservation failed. Substitute the node voltages back into every branch law; the signed current sum at each node must be zero within rounding.",
  "Find open-circuit terminal voltage for Vth. Deactivate independent voltage sources with shorts and independent current sources with opens, retain dependent sources, then find Rth with a test source if necessary. Norton current is In = Vth/Rth. Verify by predicting at least three loads and comparing with the unreduced network.",
  "τ = RC = 10,000×10 µF = 0.100 s. Charging reaches 10% at −τ ln(0.9)=10.5 ms, 63.2% at 100 ms, 90% at 230 ms, and 99% at 461 ms. After 5τ=0.5 s the remaining error is about 0.67%. Probe resistance and capacitor leakage alter the measured curve.",
  "At switch-off the coil current initially continues in the same direction. A reverse-biased diode across the energized coil becomes forward biased and provides a decay loop. Select repetitive current at least the coil current and reverse voltage above supply with margin. A plain diode gives slow release; a diode-plus-Zener raises clamp voltage and releases faster while increasing switch stress.",
  "A 2 V-peak sine has Vrms = 2/√2 = 1.414 V and Vpp = 4 V. A unipolar 5 V pulse at 25% duty has average 1.25 V and RMS 5√0.25 = 2.50 V. RMS expresses heating equivalence; average and RMS answer different questions.",
  "At 1 kHz and 1 µF, Xc = 1/(2πfC) = 159.2 Ω, so Z = 100−j159.2 Ω. Magnitude is 188.0 Ω and angle is −57.9°. With 1 Vrms applied, current magnitude is 5.32 mA and current leads source voltage by 57.9°.",
  "Choose C=10 nF and R≈1/(2π×1000×10 nF)=15.9 kΩ; 16 kΩ is a standard choice and gives 995 Hz. Ideal magnitude is 0.995 at 100 Hz, 0.707 near cutoff, and 0.0995 at 10 kHz. Recalculate with source resistance added to R and load resistance in parallel with C.",
  "Below 100 Hz the two-pole low-pass is approximately 0 dB. After 100 Hz it falls at −20 dB/decade; after 10 kHz it falls at −40 dB/decade. Each real pole contributes a phase transition from roughly one decade below to one decade above its corner, approaching −90° per pole.",
  "Measure overshoot ratio, ringing period, and settling time before changing the loop. Confirm the waveform is not probe-ground inductance or output saturation. Reduced loop bandwidth, added phase-lead/lag compensation, lower capacitive loading, or output isolation may help, but the change must be verified with gain/phase margin and load-step testing.",
  "Start with source noise density and amplifier input voltage/current noise, refer each contribution to one node, square uncorrelated terms, sum, and take the square root. Integrated white noise grows with √bandwidth, so a 100× bandwidth increase creates about 10× RMS noise. Preserve a short differential loop and restrict bandwidth before adding arbitrary capacitance.",
  "Use the datasheet I-V curve at intended current and temperature. A silicon rectifier offers low cost but more forward loss; Schottky lowers forward drop but often has higher leakage and lower reverse rating; a TVS is specified by standoff, breakdown, and clamp voltage under pulse current. The LED resistor still uses supply minus actual LED forward-voltage range.",
  "For a 12 V relay drawing 60 mA and a conservative forced β of 10, request 6 mA base current. From a 3.3 V GPIO with VBE(sat)≈0.8 V, Rb≈(3.3−0.8)/0.006=417 Ω; choose 390 or 430 Ω after checking GPIO limits. Add a coil flyback path and confirm transistor VCE and power ratings.",
  "Conduction loss is approximately I²RDS(on)×duty using RDS(on) at the actual gate voltage and hot junction. Switching loss is roughly 0.5VI(tr+tf)fs plus gate-drive loss QgVgfs. Add diode or reverse-recovery loss, then use total dissipation and thermal resistance to estimate junction temperature. Threshold voltage is not the gate-drive specification.",
  "For non-inverting gain 11, choose 1+Rf/Rg=11, for example Rg=10 kΩ and Rf=100 kΩ. Verify common-mode range at the input, output swing at the required load, GBW comfortably above 11 times signal bandwidth, and slew rate above 2πfVpeak. Check offset and bias-current error against the sensor budget.",
  "Choose lower and upper switching thresholds first, then derive the positive-feedback divider from output high/low and reference voltage. Verify startup state and source impedance. A comparator with hysteresis is preferred to a general op-amp when propagation delay, input range, output type, and recovery from saturation matter.",
  "For two-point calibration, model y = mx+b with m=(y2−y1)/(x2−x1) and b=y1−mx1. Apply the inverse relationship if converting ADC code to physical units. Keep residual error, sensor tolerance, reference error, quantization, self-heating, and calibration-range limits as separate uncertainty contributions.",
  "Create the complete truth table, mark only genuine don't-care conditions, simplify with Boolean algebra or a Karnaugh map, then verify every specified row. A multiplexer implementation assigns select inputs and maps each data input to 0, 1, or a remaining variable. Check propagation paths for static hazards.",
  "An open-drain 5 V-capable sensor can pull a 3.3 V MCU line low while the pull-up connects to 3.3 V, provided the sensor pin permits that voltage and sink current. Choose pull-up from rise-time capacitance and low-level sink limits. Synchronize asynchronous inputs with two flip-flops and debounce separately.",
  "Configure output level before enabling output mode to prevent a boot glitch. Configure input pull direction to produce a defined idle state and place current limiting outside the MCU. Keep interrupt service routines short: capture flags or timestamps, clear the source, and defer processing. A watchdog should be fed only after health checks complete.",
  "Timer frequency is fclk/(prescaler×(TOP+1)). At 48 MHz, prescaler 1 and TOP 2399 produce 20 kHz with 2400 duty steps. For an ideal 12-bit ADC with 3.3 V reference, code≈Vin/3.3×4095; 1.65 V gives about 2048. Include reference tolerance, offset, gain error, noise, and source-settling error.",
  "Replace delay-driven sequencing with a state enum, event inputs, and elapsed-time comparisons using wrap-safe unsigned subtraction. Keep hardware access behind small driver interfaces and pure decision logic in ordinary functions. Mark memory volatile only when it can change outside normal flow; volatile does not make compound operations atomic.",
  "UART is asynchronous and decoded from idle level, start bit, data order, optional parity, and stop bits. SPI requires correct clock polarity/phase and chip-select framing. I²C uses open-drain lines, pull-ups, addresses, ACK/NACK, and repeated starts. CAN requires differential wiring, arbitration, bit timing, and one termination at each physical bus end.",
  "Partition logic and load power. Use a rated driver, flyback or recirculation path, bulk and local decoupling, current limiting, and a defined enable. On reset or broken communication, outputs enter the documented safe state. Route noisy load current directly back to its source instead of through sensor or MCU reference paths.",
  "The reservoir capacitor first pass is C≈I/(2frippleΔV) for full-wave rectification. Check the ripple valley remains above LDO output plus worst-case dropout. LDO loss is (Vin−Vout)I and thermal rise is PθJA. Verify capacitor ESR/capacitance stability region, reverse-current behavior, and rectifier surge current.",
  "For an ideal buck, duty D≈Vout/Vin. Choose inductance from ΔIL=(Vin−Vout)D/(Lf), then ensure peak current Iout+ΔIL/2 stays below saturation and switch limits. Output ripple combines capacitor charge and ESR. Estimate switch, diode, inductor, capacitor, and control losses, and keep the input-switch-diode hot loop physically small.",
  "Runtime begins with usable watt-hours, not nameplate amp-hours alone: Wh≈capacity×nominal voltage×derating×conversion efficiency. Voltage sag is I×internal resistance. Define chemistry-specific charge voltage/current, low/high cutoffs, temperature limits, balancing, fuse/protection behavior, and a safe mechanical enclosure. Never parallel cells at unequal voltage.",
  "Select the bridge for stall current and supply transients, not nominal running current. Set current-sense full scale above controlled peak but below damaging current, then verify resistor pulse power and amplifier common-mode range. Insert dead time between opposing switches and provide a path for inductive and regenerative current.",
  "Daily load energy is the sum of each load power×hours, divided by conversion efficiency. Size panel energy from worst-month peak-sun hours with weather and temperature margin; size battery from required autonomy, allowable depth of discharge, temperature, and aging. Confirm cold-panel open-circuit voltage stays below controller maximum.",
  "The corrected schematic uses unique references, explicit connector pin numbers, net names that express function, visible supply and return intent, polarity and rating notes, decoupling at each IC, protection at external connectors, and test points on every rail and critical interface. ERC exceptions are documented rather than globally suppressed.",
  "Place connectors and mechanical constraints first, then protection, power conversion, decouplers, clock/reference parts, analog front end, and remaining logic. Route high-current and high-di/dt loops first over continuous return planes. Choose trace width from current and allowed temperature rise, then verify clearances, thermal spreading, test access, DRC, and fabrication limits.",
  "Do not route a fast signal across a plane split: its return current must detour, increasing loop inductance and radiation. Keep differential geometry and reference consistent, minimize stubs, add nearby return vias at layer transitions, isolate noisy clocks from ADC inputs, and connect analog/digital grounds according to converter current flow rather than slogans.",
  "At an external connector, provide the shortest low-inductance path for ESD or surge to the intended chassis/return before the disturbance enters the board. Place TVS first, then current limiting or common-mode filtering as required. Check standoff, clamp voltage at pulse current, energy, parasitics, filter resonance, and normal-signal distortion.",
  "The production matrix checks incoming identity, shorts and opens, rail resistance, current-limited power-up, programming, calibration, interfaces, load outputs, and final function. Every critical net needs accessible test strategy. Footprints are verified against package drawings and assembly process; substitutes are reviewed against electrical, thermal, mechanical, and lifecycle parameters.",
  "With excess current and missing output, do not probe randomly. Power off, inspect, compare resistance to a known-good board, and segment rails if possible. Power through a low current limit and use voltage drop or thermal evidence to localize the consuming block. Confirm the root cause by removal or isolation, then repeat the original test after repair.",
  "Use a short spring ground or differential probe for switch-node ringing; bandwidth-limit only when measuring rail ripple and state the limit. Degauss and zero the current probe. Use segmented memory or protocol triggers for intermittent traffic, enough record length for low-frequency behavior, and an electronic load with four-wire sensing where lead drop matters.",
  "Link budget in dB is transmit power + antenna gains − path/cable/mismatch losses; compare received level with receiver sensitivity plus required fade margin. Keep the antenna feed short and controlled, honor the antenna keepout, provide an uninterrupted reference, and validate in the final enclosure. Conducted power is not the same as radiated regulatory compliance.",
  "The reference IoT node samples locally, stores bounded timestamped records, and remains useful offline. Reconnection uses exponential backoff with jitter. Commands are authenticated, authorized, bounded, and fail safe. Credentials are unique and protected; updates are signed, power-fail safe, versioned, and rollback-capable. Privacy limits collection and retention.",
  "The architecture document assigns each function to hardware, bootloader, driver, service, or application state machine. Interfaces specify units, ranges, timing, ownership, timeout, and failure response. Timing and memory budgets include worst case. Watchdog recovery records reset cause, contains unsafe outputs, and preserves enough diagnostics without creating a reboot loop.",
  "Rewrite each vague goal as a measurable requirement with conditions and tolerance, then map it to analysis, inspection, simulation, or test. The verification matrix records method, equipment, configuration, pass criterion, result, and evidence link. A failed test creates a controlled issue and revision; it is never silently redefined as a pass.",
  "Use a weighted decision matrix with requirement-linked criteria such as efficiency, voltage/current capability, switching frequency, thermal path, availability, cost, tooling, maturity, EMI risk, and safety evidence. Normalize scores, perform sensitivity analysis on weights, and document vendor claims that still require prototype verification. Novelty is justified only by measurable system benefit."
];

const phases = [
  [1, 5, "Safe measurement and LED characterization"],
  [6, 10, "Passive sensor conditioning and timing board"],
  [11, 16, "Filter and low-noise signal chain"],
  [17, 22, "Analog sensor front end"],
  [23, 29, "Microcontroller data-acquisition controller"],
  [30, 34, "Protected battery and solar power system"],
  [35, 39, "Fabrication-ready mixed-signal PCB"],
  [40, 46, "Diagnosable connected embedded system"]
];

const slug = (value) => value.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-").replace(/^-|-$/g, "");
const pad = (value) => String(value).padStart(2, "0");
const json = (value) => `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\n`;

function lessonMarkdown(number, title, concepts, principle, example, fault, lessonIndex, workedSolution) {
  const groups = concepts.split(", ");
  const focus = groups.slice((lessonIndex - 1) * Math.ceil(groups.length / 3), lessonIndex * Math.ceil(groups.length / 3));
  const selected = focus.length ? focus : groups.slice(-4);
  const visual = `assets/diagrams/m${pad(number)}-overview.svg`;
  const photo = lessonIndex === 1 ? ({ 5: ["assets/components/bench-instruments.png", "Low-voltage electronics bench instruments"], 6: ["assets/components/passive-components.png", "Common passive component packages"], 17: ["assets/components/semiconductor-components.png", "Common semiconductor packages"], 25: ["assets/components/embedded-hardware.png", "Microcontrollers, sensors, displays, and connectors"] }[number] ?? null) : null;
  const photoMarkdown = photo ? `\n![${photo[1]}](${photo[0]} "${photo[1]}—identify package, polarity, ratings, and likely use before handling")\n` : "";
  const lessonFocus = lessonIndex === 1
    ? `## Physical interpretation\n\nBuild intuition before algebra. For each of **${selected.join("**, **")}**, identify what physically stores, transports, converts, or limits energy. State the relevant direction and reference: voltage exists between two nodes, current crosses a boundary, and impedance depends on frequency. A schematic symbol is a model, not the object itself; package parasitics, temperature, bias, and construction determine where that model stops being accurate.\n\nCreate a units table and dimensional check. Sketch limiting cases at zero, very small, nominal, and very large input. Limiting cases expose sign errors and impossible results before a bench test does.`
    : lessonIndex === 2
      ? `## Analysis and selection\n\nTranslate the physical model into an operating-point calculation, transfer relationship, timing budget, truth table, or energy balance. Solve symbolically first so dependencies remain visible. Then select standard parts from guaranteed limits, not marketing names. For **${selected.join("**, **")}**, compare at least two viable implementations and state why the chosen one better satisfies accuracy, power, speed, noise, cost, availability, and fault behavior.\n\nWorst-case analysis should combine supply range, tolerance, temperature, loading, and aging in the direction that stresses the requirement. Monte Carlo simulation can explore distributions, but it does not replace checking guaranteed corners.`
      : `## Measurement and failure evidence\n\nTurn the model into a measurement plan. Name the instrument, connection, range, probe attenuation, bandwidth, sample rate, trigger, reference node, expected value, acceptable range, and stop condition. Measure from source to load and from known-good block to suspect block. Preserve screenshots or tables with configuration metadata.\n\nFor **${selected.join("**, **")}**, distinguish an open, short, incorrect bias, unstable loop, noise coupling, and measurement artifact. A convincing diagnosis predicts a new observation before the measurement and survives a regression test after correction.`;
  return `# ${title}\n\n## Why this matters\n\n${principle}. In real electronics, a correct equation is only the beginning: the model must match the operating region, ratings, tolerances, measurement method, and failure conditions. This lesson connects the physical idea to a repeatable engineering workflow.\n\n![${title} concept diagram](${visual} "Module ${number} visual model: ${title}")\n${photoMarkdown}\n## Learning objectives\n\n- Explain ${selected.join(", ")} in physical and circuit terms.\n- Identify the reference directions, units, assumptions, and validity limits of the model.\n- Read the relevant graph, table, pin description, or rating in a component datasheet.\n- Predict what a safe bench measurement should show and how the instrument may affect it.\n- Recognize the characteristic evidence of ${fault}.\n\n## Core model\n\nThe central rule for this module is to **${principle}**. Begin by drawing the complete current path and naming every reference node. Mark known values with units, distinguish nominal values from limits, and state which effects are intentionally neglected. A model is useful only when its assumptions are visible.\n\nThe key vocabulary for this lesson is **${selected.join("**, **")}**. Treat each term as a measurable property rather than a word to memorize. Ask what produces it, where it is measured, what changes it, and which datasheet limit constrains it. When two descriptions appear to conflict, check whether they refer to different frequency, temperature, bias, time scale, or reference direction.\n\n${lessonFocus}\n\n## Engineering workflow\n\n1. De-energize the circuit and inspect polarity, ratings, wiring, and likely stored energy.\n2. Draw a simplified model that contains the source, return path, load, and measurement connection.\n3. Select equations from the model and carry units through every step.\n4. Calculate nominal and worst-case values using tolerances and operating limits.\n5. Predict at least two observable quantities before powering the circuit.\n6. Apply a conservative current limit and measure from the source outward.\n7. Compare evidence with prediction; revise one assumption at a time.\n\n## Worked example\n\n${example}\n\n### Reference resolution\n\n${workedSolution}\n\nA defensible solution identifies the unknown, writes the governing relationship symbolically, substitutes values with units, checks order of magnitude, selects a realizable component or setting, and verifies ratings. It then predicts the measurement range rather than a single perfect number. Component tolerance, instrument accuracy, loading, temperature, and wiring resistance explain small differences; a large difference requires a new hypothesis.\n\n## Datasheet and component judgment\n\nNever select a part from its headline value alone. Check absolute maximum ratings, recommended operating conditions, typical versus guaranteed performance, temperature range, package and thermal path, frequency dependence, tolerance, lifetime or drift, and test conditions. Typical values help predict behavior; guaranteed limits decide whether a design is safe. Derate voltage, current, power, and temperature when failure has meaningful consequences.\n\n## Common mistakes and fault evidence\n\nA likely fault pattern is **${fault}**. Do not begin by replacing parts. First reproduce the symptom safely, record supply current, divide the system into functional blocks, and measure the boundary between a known-good and suspect block. Opens usually preserve voltage on one side while preventing current; shorts collapse voltage and raise current; bias errors shift a waveform; noise and oscillation require attention to bandwidth, grounding, return paths, and probe technique.\n\n> **Warning:** Keep course labs at extra-low voltage unless a qualified supervisor and appropriate isolated equipment are present. Never connect a bench oscilloscope ground clip to an unknown mains-referenced circuit.\n\n## Guided practice\n\n- Redraw the visual with reference directions and expected polarities.\n- Find one real component datasheet and record three guaranteed limits plus their test conditions.\n- Calculate one nominal result and one worst-case range.\n- Write the exact meter or oscilloscope setup needed to test the prediction safely.\n- Explain which single measurement best distinguishes ${fault} from a healthy circuit.\n\n## Recap\n\nReliable electronics work combines a physical model, unit-aware calculation, datasheet limits, safe measurement, and evidence-based troubleshooting. Preserve all five in the engineering notebook so another person can reproduce the result.\n`;
}

function analysisExerciseType(number) {
  if ([1, 2, 41, 42, 45].includes(number)) return "shortAnswer";
  if ([9, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 20, 21, 30, 31].includes(number)) return "simulation";
  if ([25, 26, 27, 28, 29, 43, 44].includes(number)) return "firmware";
  if ([35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 46].includes(number)) return "designReview";
  if ([3, 7, 8, 10, 18, 19, 23, 24, 32, 33, 34].includes(number)) return "schematic";
  return "calculation";
}

function assessment(number, title, concepts, principle, example, fault, phaseProject, workedSolution) {
  const id = `m${pad(number)}-assessment`;
  const mainConcept = concepts.split(", ")[0];
  const exercises = [
    {
      id: `m${pad(number)}-concept-check`, type: "multipleChoice", title: `${title}: engineering judgment`,
      instructionMarkdown: `Which approach best demonstrates sound judgment when working with ${mainConcept}?`,
      choices: [
        `State assumptions, calculate a bounded expectation, check ratings, and verify with a safe measurement`,
        `Use the typical value as a guarantee and increase power until the expected result appears`,
        `Replace components at random before documenting the original symptom`
      ], correctChoice: 0,
      hints: ["The strongest answer preserves safety, assumptions, limits, and evidence."]
    },
    {
      id: `m${pad(number)}-worked-analysis`, type: analysisExerciseType(number), title: `${title}: worked analysis`,
      instructionMarkdown: `${example}\n\nShow assumptions, equations, units, standard-value choices, ratings, tolerance effects, and the measurements that would verify the result.`,
      checklist: ["I drew or described the complete source and return path.", "I carried units and checked the order of magnitude.", "I checked ratings and at least one worst-case condition.", "I wrote an observable verification step before viewing the solution."],
      hints: ["Start with a symbolic relationship before substituting numbers.", "Separate typical behavior from guaranteed limits and add measurement tolerance."],
      solutionMarkdown: `## Reference solution\n\n${workedSolution}\n\nThe governing principle is: **${principle}**. Define every reference direction and convert values to coherent SI units. Compare the result with component ratings and standard available values, then report a bounded expectation that includes tolerance, temperature, supply variation, and instrument loading.\n\n### Verification evidence\n\n1. Power off and confirm wiring, polarity, and discharge state.\n2. Configure a conservative current limit.\n3. Predict source current and the critical node voltages or waveform features.\n4. Power on and compare the first measurement with the bounded prediction.\n5. Stop if current, temperature, smell, or waveform exceeds the safe bound.\n\nThe notebook should make both the reasoning and bench test independently reproducible.`
    },
    {
      id: `m${pad(number)}-fault-lab`, type: number % 4 === 0 ? "troubleshooting" : "lab", title: `${title}: measurement and fault investigation`,
      instructionMarkdown: `Plan or perform a low-voltage investigation of **${fault}**. Record a safe setup, predicted healthy evidence, observed evidence, one hypothesis at a time, and the final verification.`,
      checklist: ["I documented the de-energized inspection and safe current limit.", "I recorded expected healthy readings before measuring.", "I used one falsifiable hypothesis at a time.", "I included final evidence that distinguishes cause from symptom."],
      hints: ["Measure the supply current and block boundaries before probing internal details.", "Change only one condition between measurements."],
      solutionMarkdown: `## Reference investigation\n\nStart de-energized. Inspect orientation, solder joints, connector pinout, component values, and evidence of heat. Check resistance only where stored charge and parallel paths are understood. Apply power through a current-limited extra-low-voltage source.\n\nThe first evidence set is supply voltage, supply current, and the input/output of each functional block. Compare each reading with the predicted range. The fault **${fault}** should be accepted only when a measurement distinguishes it from alternatives. After repair, repeat the original reproduction steps and a nearby edge case.\n\n### Required record\n\n- symptom and conditions;\n- annotated schematic or block diagram;\n- instrument, probe, range, bandwidth, and reference;\n- expected and measured values;\n- hypothesis and discriminating test;\n- root cause, corrective action, and regression check.`
    }
  ];
  if (phaseProject) exercises.push({
    id: `m${pad(number)}-phase-project`, type: "project", title: phaseProject,
    instructionMarkdown: `Complete the phase project **${phaseProject}**. Produce requirements, safety limits, calculations, schematic or block diagram, component choices, test plan, measured or simulated evidence, fault analysis, and a concise design review.`,
    checklist: ["Requirements and pass criteria are measurable.", "Safety, power, thermal, and interface limits are documented.", "Calculations and component selections cite evidence.", "The test plan includes nominal, boundary, and fault cases.", "Results include annotated diagrams or waveforms and a revision note."],
    hints: ["Freeze measurable requirements before selecting components.", "Review the current loops, return paths, fault states, and test access before layout or assembly."],
    solutionMarkdown: `## Reference project standard\n\nA passing project is not defined by matching one exact circuit. It is defined by traceable requirements and evidence. The reference architecture separates source protection, power conversion, sensing, control, interfaces, and loads. Each block states input/output ranges, safe states, and test points.\n\nThe calculation package includes nominal and worst-case values, power and thermal budgets, tolerance effects, and component-rating margins. The verification matrix maps every requirement to a test or inspection with objective pass criteria. The troubleshooting section includes at least one injected open, short or bias fault and the measurements that isolate it.\n\nA strong submission also records a discovered weakness and the revision made in response; engineering quality is demonstrated by the feedback loop, not by pretending the first version was perfect.`
  });
  if (number === 46) exercises.push({
    id: "m46-final-capstone", type: "project", title: "Final Electronics Engineering Capstone",
    instructionMarkdown: "Design and defend a substantial low-voltage electronic system integrating analog sensing, embedded control, communications, protected power, PCB considerations, diagnostics, and objective verification. Include requirements, safety analysis, calculations, schematic, simulations, firmware architecture, layout review, measurements, fault injection, and revision history.",
    checklist: ["Every requirement maps to objective verification evidence.", "The safety, power, thermal, interface, and fault-state analyses are complete.", "Schematics, simulations, firmware, and layout decisions are mutually consistent.", "Bring-up and troubleshooting records include annotated measurements.", "The design defense states limitations, residual risks, and next revisions."],
    hints: ["Choose a problem whose requirements can be measured with available low-voltage equipment.", "Freeze interfaces and power budgets before detailed schematic and firmware work."],
    solutionMarkdown: "## Capstone reference standard\n\nThere is no single correct product, but there is a correct evidence structure. The reference submission contains measurable requirements, a block diagram with interface ranges, a complete power and energy budget, worst-case analog calculations, protected external interfaces, deterministic firmware states, an offline-safe communications policy, reviewed return paths and decoupling, and a verification matrix.\n\nBring-up proceeds rail by rail under current limit, then clock/reset, programming, static I/O, sensors, actuators, and communications. Each stage has expected readings and a stop condition. Fault injection demonstrates recovery from a disconnected sensor, shorted output or communication failure without unsafe behavior. The final defense distinguishes verified facts, simulations, assumptions, and work still required for certification or high-energy use."
  });
  return { id, title: `${title}: assessment and deliberate practice`, theoryFile: `lessons/module-${pad(number)}/m${pad(number)}-assessment.md`, exercises };
}

function assessmentMarkdown(number, title) {
  return `# ${title}: assessment and deliberate practice\n\nUse this assessment to connect theory, calculation, visual reasoning, measurement, and troubleshooting. Work without the solution first. Keep units visible, state assumptions, and treat every absolute maximum as a boundary rather than an operating target.\n\n## Submission standard\n\nA strong response is reproducible by another learner. It contains a labeled model or schematic, calculations with units, component and instrument settings, predicted evidence, measured or simulated evidence, uncertainty, and a short conclusion that distinguishes fact from inference.\n\n> **Tip:** If the result disagrees with prediction, preserve the disagreement. Check references, polarity, units, loading, bandwidth, and operating region before changing the circuit.\n`;
}

function overviewSvg(number, title, concepts) {
  const labels = concepts.split(", ").slice(0, 4);
  const boxes = labels.map((label, index) => `<g><rect x="${70 + index * 205}" y="150" width="165" height="92" rx="12" fill="#172033" stroke="#6ee7b7" stroke-width="2"/><text x="${152 + index * 205}" y="190" fill="#f8fafc" font-size="15" text-anchor="middle">${label.replaceAll("&", "&amp;")}</text><text x="${152 + index * 205}" y="216" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="12" text-anchor="middle">measure • model • verify</text>${index < 3 ? `<path d="M ${235 + index * 205} 196 H ${270 + index * 205}" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="3" marker-end="url(#arrow)"/>` : ""}</g>`).join("");
  return `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 920 360" role="img" aria-labelledby="title desc"><title id="title">Module ${number}: ${title}</title><desc id="desc">Four-stage engineering model connecting ${labels.join(", ")}</desc><defs><marker id="arrow" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="7" refY="4" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L8,4 L0,8 Z" fill="#f59e0b"/></marker></defs><rect width="920" height="360" fill="#0b1020"/><text x="460" y="55" fill="#f8fafc" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="25" text-anchor="middle">${title.replaceAll("&", "&amp;")}</text><text x="460" y="86" fill="#94a3b8" font-family="Arial,sans-serif" font-size="14" text-anchor="middle">physical behavior → circuit model → bounded prediction → evidence</text>${boxes}<path d="M70 285 H850" stroke="#334155" stroke-width="2"/><circle cx="120" cy="285" r="8" fill="#60a5fa"/><text x="135" y="290" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="13">safe setup</text><circle cx="350" cy="285" r="8" fill="#a78bfa"/><text x="365" y="290" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="13">units and limits</text><circle cx="585" cy="285" r="8" fill="#f59e0b"/><text x="600" y="290" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="13">measurement</text><circle cx="785" cy="285" r="8" fill="#6ee7b7"/><text x="800" y="290" fill="#cbd5e1" font-size="13">revision</text></svg>`;
}

await mkdir(root, { recursive: true });
await mkdir(resolve(root, "content"), { recursive: true });
await mkdir(resolve(root, "projects"), { recursive: true });
await mkdir(resolve(root, "assets/diagrams"), { recursive: true });
await mkdir(resolve(root, "assets/components"), { recursive: true });

const modulePaths = [];
const projectPaths = [];
for (let index = 0; index < modules.length; index += 1) {
  const number = index + 1;
  const [title, concepts, principle, example, fault] = modules[index];
  const workedSolution = workedSolutions[index];
  const moduleSlug = slug(title);
  const modulePath = `content/module-${pad(number)}-${moduleSlug}.json`;
  modulePaths.push(modulePath);
  const phaseIndex = phases.findIndex(([, end]) => end === number);
  const phase = phaseIndex >= 0 ? phases[phaseIndex] : undefined;
  const lessons = [];
  for (let lessonIndex = 1; lessonIndex <= 3; lessonIndex += 1) {
    const lessonTitle = lessonIndex === 1 ? `${title}: physical model and vocabulary` : lessonIndex === 2 ? `${title}: analysis and component judgment` : `${title}: measurement, design, and failure evidence`;
    const lessonId = `m${pad(number)}-l${pad(lessonIndex)}-${slug(lessonTitle).slice(0, 52)}`;
    const theoryFile = `lessons/module-${pad(number)}/${lessonId}.md`;
    lessons.push({ id: lessonId, title: lessonTitle, theoryFile, exercises: [] });
    await mkdir(resolve(root, `lessons/module-${pad(number)}`), { recursive: true });
    await writeFile(resolve(root, theoryFile), lessonMarkdown(number, lessonTitle, concepts, principle, example, fault, lessonIndex, workedSolution), "utf8");
  }
  const phaseProject = phase?.[2];
  lessons.push(assessment(number, title, concepts, principle, example, fault, phaseProject, workedSolution));
  await writeFile(resolve(root, `lessons/module-${pad(number)}/m${pad(number)}-assessment.md`), assessmentMarkdown(number, title), "utf8");
  await writeFile(resolve(root, modulePath), json({ id: `module-${pad(number)}-${moduleSlug}`, title: `Module ${number}: ${title}`, description: `${principle}. Covers ${concepts}.`, lessons }), "utf8");
  await writeFile(resolve(root, `assets/diagrams/m${pad(number)}-overview.svg`), overviewSvg(number, title, concepts), "utf8");
  if (phaseProject) {
    const phaseNumber = phaseIndex + 1;
    const projectPath = `projects/phase-${phaseNumber}-${slug(phaseProject)}.json`;
    projectPaths.push(projectPath);
    const phaseStart = phase[0];
    await writeFile(resolve(root, projectPath), json({ id: `electronics-phase-${phaseNumber}-project`, title: phaseProject, descriptionMarkdown: `# ${phaseProject}\n\nIntegrate the theory and practical evidence from modules ${phaseStart}–${number}. Submit a safe, reproducible engineering package rather than only a working artifact.`, learningObjectives: ["Translate requirements into a circuit or system architecture", "Use calculations and datasheet limits to select components", "Plan safe measurements and objective verification", "Diagnose at least one injected or simulated fault", "Document evidence and revisions"], checkpointExerciseIds: [`m${pad(number)}-phase-project`] }), "utf8");
  }
}

const capstonePath = "projects/final-electronics-capstone.json";
projectPaths.push(capstonePath);
await writeFile(resolve(root, capstonePath), json({ id: "final-electronics-capstone", title: "Final Electronics Engineering Capstone", descriptionMarkdown: "# Final Electronics Engineering Capstone\n\nDesign a substantial low-voltage electronic system such as a solar environmental monitor, battery-powered controller, motor controller, measurement instrument, or industrial sensor node. Deliver measurable requirements, safety analysis, architecture, calculations, schematic, simulations, firmware design, PCB review, bring-up plan, verification matrix, annotated measurements, fault injection, EMI and thermal review, and a design defense.", learningObjectives: ["Integrate analog, digital, embedded, power, layout, and troubleshooting skills", "Produce traceable engineering evidence", "Design safe states and fault containment", "Defend tradeoffs and limitations honestly"], checkpointExerciseIds: ["m46-final-capstone"] }), "utf8");

await writeFile(resolve(root, "manifest.json"), json({ format: "learnpack", schemaVersion: "1.0.0", id: "electronics-engineering-foundations", version: "1.0.0", course: { title: "Electronics Engineering Foundations: Theory to Practical Systems", description: "A complete offline electronics curriculum covering safety, circuit theory, components, AC and frequency response, semiconductor and analog design, digital logic, microcontrollers, communications, power electronics, batteries and solar, schematic and PCB layout, signal integrity, EMI, RF, IoT, measurement, troubleshooting, verification, and capstone engineering practice.", language: "electronics", languageVersion: "1.0", level: "beginner", estimatedHours: 520, authors: [{ name: "LearnLocal Electronics Academy" }] }, runtime: { adapter: "electronics", adapterRange: ">=0.1.0 <2.0.0", runtimeVersion: "1.0", containerRequirements: "Study-mode electronics curriculum. Optional low-voltage bench equipment, SPICE simulator, KiCad, and Arduino-compatible development tools are used in guided activities; no course content can install or execute them." }, modules: modulePaths, projects: projectPaths }), "utf8");

console.log(`Built electronics curriculum: ${modules.length} modules, ${modules.length * 4} lessons, ${modules.length * 3 + phases.length + 1} exercises, ${projectPaths.length} projects.`);
